1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,501
Your tool now receives input from a model.

2
00:00:03,851 --> 00:00:09,218
And here's a habit that separates hobby
projects from production code: never trust

3
00:00:09,218 --> 00:00:09,938
that input.

4
00:00:10,288 --> 00:00:11,780
Always check it first.

5
00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:17,810
In this lesson, you'll see what can go
wrong with tool input, what strict mode does

6
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:23,558
and doesn't protect you from, and how to
turn every mistake into a message the model

7
00:00:23,558 --> 00:00:24,106
can fix.

8
00:00:25,906 --> 00:00:28,629
You'll explain why
tool input is untrusted.

9
00:00:29,229 --> 00:00:32,421
Know what strict mode
guarantees, and what it doesn't.

10
00:00:33,021 --> 00:00:36,801
Validate input with
Pydantic, before running a tool.

11
00:00:37,401 --> 00:00:41,336
And return clear errors, so
the model can correct itself.

12
00:00:43,136 --> 00:00:44,617
Why validate at all?

13
00:00:44,967 --> 00:00:46,452
Think of a bank clerk.

14
00:00:46,802 --> 00:00:48,791
You hand them a form to send money.

15
00:00:49,141 --> 00:00:51,110
Before anything moves, they check it.

16
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:53,703
Is the account number the right length?

17
00:00:54,053 --> 00:00:55,366
Is the date real?

18
00:00:55,716 --> 00:00:57,407
Is the amount positive?

19
00:00:57,757 --> 00:01:00,957
If something's wrong, they
don't crash, and they don't guess.

20
00:01:01,307 --> 00:01:05,123
They hand the form back, and
tell you exactly what to fix.

21
00:01:05,473 --> 00:01:07,716
Your tools should behave the same way.

22
00:01:08,316 --> 00:01:13,741
Tool input comes from a model, and the
model's input comes, partly, from users.

23
00:01:14,091 --> 00:01:18,993
A model can misread a date,
invent a value, or be tricked by a user.

24
00:01:19,343 --> 00:01:23,661
So treat tool input like any
untrusted input from the internet.

25
00:01:25,462 --> 00:01:30,098
There are two layers of protection,
and it's important to know which does what.

26
00:01:30,448 --> 00:01:35,314
Strict mode makes sure the right
fields are there, with no unknown fields.

27
00:01:35,914 --> 00:01:40,320
It makes sure the types are right,
and that enum values are from the list.

28
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:45,901
But limits, like a maximum length or
a minimum number, aren't part of strict

29
00:01:45,901 --> 00:01:46,426
schemas.

30
00:01:46,776 --> 00:01:48,840
Pydantic checks those in your code.

31
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:55,297
And business rules, like the due date not
being in the past, or task seven existing,

32
00:01:55,297 --> 00:01:57,040
only your code can check.

33
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:01,009
Here's how the project validates.

34
00:02:01,359 --> 00:02:06,897
Pydantic's model validate takes the
raw dictionary from the model, and either

35
00:02:06,897 --> 00:02:10,852
returns a checked object,
or raises a validation error.

36
00:02:11,452 --> 00:02:13,274
If it fails, we don't crash.

37
00:02:13,624 --> 00:02:19,249
We turn every problem into one short
line, the field name and what's wrong, and

38
00:02:19,249 --> 00:02:20,602
raise a tool error.

39
00:02:20,952 --> 00:02:26,778
Remember from Module 4: a tool error
becomes an observation the model can read.

40
00:02:28,578 --> 00:02:31,741
Some rules need context
that a schema can't know.

41
00:02:32,091 --> 00:02:33,917
Is the due date in the past?

42
00:02:34,267 --> 00:02:35,775
That depends on today.

43
00:02:36,125 --> 00:02:42,045
So that check lives inside the tool, and
it raises a tool error with both dates, so

44
00:02:42,045 --> 00:02:44,898
the model knows exactly what went wrong.

45
00:02:46,698 --> 00:02:51,823
Let's send five inputs through the real
run tool function, and see what happens to

46
00:02:51,823 --> 00:02:52,385
each one.

47
00:02:52,985 --> 00:02:57,084
The first input is valid, so
the task is added, as task one.

48
00:02:57,684 --> 00:03:00,370
The second has a due date of next friday.

49
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:05,885
That's not a date in the right format,
so we get a clear message about the due

50
00:03:05,885 --> 00:03:06,282
field.

51
00:03:06,882 --> 00:03:12,257
The third says urgent, which isn't one
of the allowed priorities, and the message

52
00:03:12,257 --> 00:03:13,915
lists the three that are.

53
00:03:14,515 --> 00:03:18,514
The fourth has an empty title,
which breaks the minimum length.

54
00:03:19,114 --> 00:03:24,766
And the fifth has a perfectly valid
shape, but the date is in January, already in

55
00:03:24,766 --> 00:03:25,394
the past.

56
00:03:25,744 --> 00:03:27,775
Only the business rule catches it.

57
00:03:29,575 --> 00:03:32,154
Now the most useful part: recovery.

58
00:03:32,504 --> 00:03:35,117
Turn one: the model sends a bad call.

59
00:03:35,467 --> 00:03:40,902
Our code returns an error result,
marked is error, that names both problems.

60
00:03:41,502 --> 00:03:47,181
Turn two: the model reads the message,
fixes both fields, and the call succeeds.

61
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:50,423
Turn three: the model answers the user.

62
00:03:52,223 --> 00:03:55,339
Let's watch that
recovery in the real agent.

63
00:03:55,689 --> 00:03:57,355
First, an empty store.

64
00:03:57,955 --> 00:04:02,052
Here the offline fake is
scripted to send a bad call first.

65
00:04:02,402 --> 00:04:05,070
Turn one: the error, with both problems.

66
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:08,612
Turn two: the corrected
call, and the task is added.

67
00:04:08,962 --> 00:04:14,803
With a real model, this recovery isn't
scripted: the model reads your message and

68
00:04:14,803 --> 00:04:16,462
fixes the input itself.

69
00:04:16,812 --> 00:04:19,386
That's why clear messages matter so much.

70
00:04:21,186 --> 00:04:22,503
Pause and decide.

71
00:04:22,853 --> 00:04:27,379
For each problem, which layer
catches it: strict mode, or your code?

72
00:04:27,979 --> 00:04:28,618
One.

73
00:04:28,968 --> 00:04:33,187
The model sends a field called
deadline, that isn't in the schema.

74
00:04:33,787 --> 00:04:34,409
Two.

75
00:04:34,759 --> 00:04:39,700
The model asks to complete task
forty-two, but there are only three tasks.

76
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:40,997
Three.

77
00:04:41,347 --> 00:04:46,049
A note's text is five thousand
characters, and the limit is two thousand.

78
00:04:52,649 --> 00:04:56,394
One: strict mode, because
additional properties is false.

79
00:04:56,744 --> 00:05:01,264
Two: your code, because only
the store knows which tasks exist.

80
00:05:01,614 --> 00:05:06,732
Three: your code too, because length
limits aren't part of strict schemas, so

81
00:05:06,732 --> 00:05:08,128
Pydantic checks them.

82
00:05:09,928 --> 00:05:11,069
Interview corner.

83
00:05:11,419 --> 00:05:16,164
If you use strict tool schemas, do
you still need to validate tool inputs?

84
00:05:18,764 --> 00:05:19,449
Yes.

85
00:05:19,799 --> 00:05:22,561
Strict mode guarantees
the shape and the types.

86
00:05:22,911 --> 00:05:28,647
It can't check limits that strict schemas
leave out, or business rules, like the due

87
00:05:28,647 --> 00:05:32,267
date being in the future,
or task forty-two existing.

88
00:05:32,617 --> 00:05:34,899
The trap is saying strict means correct.

89
00:05:35,249 --> 00:05:36,869
Strict means well-formed.

90
00:05:37,469 --> 00:05:41,673
Follow-up: what should a tool
return when its input is invalid?

91
00:05:44,273 --> 00:05:49,803
A tool result marked is error, with a
short, specific message: which field is wrong,

92
00:05:49,803 --> 00:05:51,054
and what's allowed.

93
00:05:51,404 --> 00:05:53,433
Then the model can fix the call.

94
00:05:53,783 --> 00:05:59,296
The trap: crashing the loop with an
exception, or a vague message like invalid

95
00:05:59,296 --> 00:06:01,840
input, which the model can't act on.

96
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:04,499
Quick check.

97
00:06:04,849 --> 00:06:08,436
Which of these can only
your code catch, not strict mode?

98
00:06:14,636 --> 00:06:16,832
A due date that's already in the past.

99
00:06:17,182 --> 00:06:19,241
It's a perfectly well-formed date.

100
00:06:19,591 --> 00:06:22,268
Only your code knows today, and the rule.

101
00:06:24,068 --> 00:06:24,876
Recap.

102
00:06:25,226 --> 00:06:29,612
Tool input is untrusted, so
check it before running anything.

103
00:06:30,212 --> 00:06:32,376
Strict mode covers shape and types.

104
00:06:32,726 --> 00:06:35,523
Your code covers
limits and business rules.

105
00:06:36,123 --> 00:06:40,969
Pydantic's model validate turns
raw input into a checked object.

106
00:06:41,569 --> 00:06:46,441
And error messages name the field and
what's allowed, so the model can fix it.

107
00:06:47,041 --> 00:06:50,536
Next lesson: tools that
are slow, or never answer.

108
00:06:50,886 --> 00:06:54,931
You'll add timeouts, so one
stuck tool can't freeze your agent.
